Date:     Mon, 3 Jan 94 2:50:58 GMT
From: nancy@genie.slhs.udel.edu
Subject: Khmer Vert - Seal Rant

I'm in substantial agreement with Tim May about environmental issues
not being well covered in libertarian theory--I'm going to throw in
a few more thoughts and questions.

I don't see that libertarian theory makes any provision for people
who want to preserve wilderness, since the only way to claim land
is to change it, and at that point it isn't wilderness. I asked
Murray Rothbard about this, and he couldn't understand why anyone
would want wilderness.

Another question is how to handle large scale changes in climate
that are caused by the actions of many people. To take an example
where the science is solid, cities generate a lot of heat and 
produce updrafts that attract storms. If someone is hurt in
a storm that was pulled into their area by a nearby city, are
they owed anything by the inhabitants who've been generating
heat?

In that case, at least the science is solid, so far as I know--
what about cases where the science is questionable but plausible?
(Even if you don't think that the ozone hole is a problem, I
think it's likely that, as people have more impact and as there's
a better scientific understanding of how the planet works, more
cases like the ozone hole will turn up.)

Here's a problem of the commons--antibiotics. If antibiotics are
available over the counter, people are going to be breeding
resistant strains of bacteria fairly quickly. Should there be
laws and/or social pressure to get people to use antibiotics
moderately?  Or would it be both reasonable and simpler to say
that people don't have a right to have any particular antibiotic
work, and therefore resistant bacteria aren't anybody's responsibility?

Nancy Lebovitz

