>On Fri, 31 Dec 1993, Timothy C. May wrote:
>> 
>> The environmental issues are unresolved, I think. Rand never had to
>> deal with it (except for her trademark "Factories belching smoke are
>> the symbol of man's control over nature"), and, as I recall,
>> "Machinery of Freedom" did not dwell on issues of overfishing of the
>> oceans or loss of habitat or the ozone layer in detail. (David can
>> correct me if I've misremembered this.)

We have a whole section on libertarian environmentalism here at Freedom's
Forum.  Terry Anderson & Donald Leal's "Free Market Environmentalism"
is the standard introductory work on the subject.  There's a lot of
R&D that's been done in this area, and its growing.

>> The issues are familiar: 
>> 
>> - property rights for things that have historically not been
>> considered property (air, oceans, species, etc.). Challenging.
>
>And how can someone claim ownership of these?

May's statement should have read "have, with some significant
exceptions we can learn from, historically..."  From the ISIL Pamphlet
"The Pollution Solution":

"The British long ago learned how to stop pollution of their rivers.
Fishing rights in British streams and rivers are a private good that can
be bought and sold.  For the last century, polluters have been routinely
dragged into the courts by angry owners and forced to rectify any damage
they may have caused.  Every owner on these rivers has in fact become an
environmental protector - because each stands to profit from nurturing the
environment.

"In the Gulf of Mexico, shrimp fishermen once claimed parts of the ocean
as their property in the time-honored practice of homesteading.  They
formed a voluntary association to keep the waters productive and to avoid
overfishing - until the U.S. government took over as caretaker in the early
1900s."

C'est une fait accompli.  The oceans could be fenced off profitably if the
State would get out of the way.  The technology already exists: nets.  They
will be the barbed wire of the high sea fish farms of the future.

Endangered species are already being preserved for profit by ranches that
harvest them for exotic food restaurants.  Antelopes as well as elephants
are benefiting from this, as are other species.  BTW, it's worthwhile
noting that only African elephants are endangered, where they're State-
owned.  Indian elephants have been privately owned for centuries, and
they're thriving.

>Hard to say, but I'm inclined
>to say that ownership of property should be thought of more by the amount
>of work someone (or some entity has put into it).

Labor theory of property.  All it takes is for someone to make it into
capital, even if that takes little work.  You make it into capital, it's
your property.

>> - can libertarians support "rationing" of a service or commodity?

All scarce goods are rationed.  The only question is whether they're going
to be rationed by the price system or by the State.  Libertarians as
libertarians can't support State rationing without contradiction.

>> My problem here is that since no one owns the supply
>> of snow leopards or seal pups or clean air, how is a market ever
>> established?)

Claim 'em.  Enforce your claim.  Doesn't the Chinese government own the
snow leopards?  Buy 'em.  They need the capital.

Also, zoos exist for the preservation of many animal species, including
endangered ones such as pandas.  The best zoo I've ever been too, the
San Diego Wild Animal Park, is entirely privately owned and operated.
Many zoos are - the Philadelphia Zoo is another example.

The real problem comes in when you have animals like pandas that have a
hard time in captivity because they die or fail to reproduce.  Then you
can't use zoos to preserve them, you have to preserve them in their
wild habitat - which you could still do by fencing it off and charging
admission, like they do at wild game preserves in Africa.  If only the
Chinese government would get a clue!

>If I suddenly find that the increased pollution
>in the air is a result of car pollution, how do I change this? I should be
>able to sue the manufacturer's of the vehicles for failing to ensure that
>their products aren't health hazards, and so, endanger my health (or would I
>sue the driver of such a vehicle?) Of course, this would completely screw up
>the legal system I think, since everyone would start filing charges.

Adopt the loser-pays rule and watch most of those claims get thrown out of
court for being based upon junk science, with the plaintiffs having to foot
the bill of the defendants.

>I haven't thought a lot about punishment of rights-breakers (I'm trying to get
>away from the word criminal since it seems to me to be a loaded term, full of
>state rhetoric),

Try "tortfeasors."

>The problem seems to be that the poacher's don't know a lot about real free
>market economics, and that they are killing there own market.

Nope.  They're behaving perfectly rationally, given their environment, in
which private ownership and use of elephants/rhinos is prohibited.  Private
disposal of them is also prohibited, but tough to enforce, so that's what
poachers do: dispose of them, for profit.  Sure, it's unsustainable, but
what better options are they permitted?

>(I assume that
>the market for poached products like ivory is competitive.)

False.  It's not a free market, but a black one.

>Is there any way
>to make it more profitable, in the short and long run, to not poach?

Yah.  Privatize the animals.

>The question seems to become: At what point does
>exploitation of a resource become wrong? (I mean exploitation here as using
>a resource for doing useful things).

When it becomes more costly than the alternatives.

>At what point does waste become serious
>pollution (face it, carbon dioxide is waste. In large amounts we would die
>from it, but we breath it out all the time. When does exhaust become "bad"?)

When the dosage gets high enough to cause provable harm, then it becomes a
