>    The Coase Theorem probably applies here. I recall my microecon
>teacher giving a 'classic example' of a chemical plant upstream
>polluting a river which has a fishing club down stream. The fishing
>club can naturally solve this problem by compensating the chemical
>plant for the cost of their externality. (met with by loud
>protest from the class "but that's not fair", "it's blackmail",
>"a protection racket", etc)
>
>    But this is a good solution.

The Coase theorem works both ways, no matter how property rights are
assigned.  In the example you give, the chem plant is asigned rights
to pollute downstream.  This is what evokes the negative reaction
of your classmates.

Think about it, though: how did the chem plant acquire those rights?
They didn't make the downstream into capital, the fishermen did.  The
only way the chem plant could have those rights is by State fiat.
This would be the positivist's notion of rights.  On the other hand,
if the right not to be polluted (defined as the imposition of unreason-
able harm/costs) is "assigned" to the fishermen and their part of
the stream, then the chem plant merely compensates them for any harm
done.  Same efficient outcome from the point of view of social cost,
with reverse distributive consequences.

>You like squirrels, you pay
>land owners for the opportunity cost they face of not developing
>the little squirrel nests into strip malls.

If they own the land which constitutes their habitat.  If squirrel-lovers
acquire it first, then the developers agree not to disturb their habitat
in exchange for development rights, if possible.

One informative way to look at the Endangered Species act is to call it
the "habitat nationalization act," because it's used mostly to stop
the development of land by their private owners than to actually preserve
endangered species.  The Northern Spotted Owl lives fine in a habitat of
second-growth forest, for example.  The eco-fascists don't care.  They
just want to preserve old-growth forest, not the owls per se.  So, they
oughta buy the land.

