              > Maybe we need cryonics for almost extinct species. 
        

You wouldn't need anything as complex and expensive as cryonics
for this.

Every cell of a body (except red blood cells in mammals)
contains the complete genetic instructions for the entire
individual. It would be very simple to store a few cells , they
would not even have to be frozen, a fixation solution such as
glutaraldehyde would render the animals genetic code readable
for centuries at least and probably much more.

I would like to see this done for humans as well. I realize that
none of the individuals memories would be preserved and so it is
far inferior to  present methods, but I also realize it would be
enormously cheaper and easier and it could be done while the
person is still alive. You only need one cell so think of it as
a Cryonics starter kit. Contrary to popular opinion survival is
probably not a all or nothing matter and for those who can not
afford the present options, and as a backup for those who can,
it would be far better than nothing.


