Harvey Newstrom writes

>Most of this talk seems to be about where in the U.S. to live.
>Does anybody have any knowledge or opinions about places outside the U.S.?

I've spent a small amount of time in Singapore and found it to be a
surprisingly congenial place for a foreigner, which is odd considering that it
is basically an authoritarian socialist dictatorship.

It's clean.  It's modern.  The cost of living is low (by my standards).  The
food is wonderful.  The people are attractive and very friendly.

There *are* lots and lots of rules, with draconian enforcement.  However, most
of them have to do with things that people should be shot for anyway (like
pissing in the street or littering) or things that I am not interested in (like
drugs).  The glaring exception is that they don't look too kindly on people who
criticize the government (though Singaporeans I met did seem to be comfortable
talking freely about stuff that their government did which *they* thought was
stupid, they tended to focus on different things than I would have).  On the
other hand, if you don't like it you are free to leave, which is more than can
be said for many places in the world where you might get into trouble.  As a
small island city-state, its scale is such that the kind of socialist
infrastructure management that they are prone to actually works pretty well.
In a recent article in Wired, William Gibson called it "Disneyland with the
death penalty", which sums it up the situation rather nicely (except that it's
cleaner than Disneyland).

On the other hand, for the entrepreneur there seems to be substantially more
economic freedom there than there is here in the U.S.  All the people I met
there seemed to be infused with an entreprenurial spirit that would greatly
enrich the world if it could be bottled and exported.  Everybody, even the temp
girls who were hired to mostly be decorative at the trade show booth whose
setup brought me there in the first place, seemed to be working some kind of
venture or another.  A denser cluster of genuine running dog capitalists I have
never encountered.

I think there is a lesson in this somewhere.  Many of the things which we find
objectionable when done by a government we don't find objectionable if they are
done as a matter of voluntary contract by private parties (e.g., zoning laws
vs. a real estate developer's "planned community").  Although I find many of
the things that the Singapore government does stupid or immoral, I'm not nearly
as bothered by them as I would be if the U.S (or California or Palo Alto)
government even proposed to do the same things.  Singapore feels much more like
the big private real estate development that you can buy into or not, as you
choose.  I'm not sure if it's the scale or if it's that I'm an outsider there.


